Z||000013||olympic||12-26-1999||03:02 PM||||   I personally am a little concerned.  Our provincial power company is not letting any of its employees take holidays in december or january even though they say they are ready. Myself, I'll be ok. I live on a farm with its own water supply and my furnace burns diesel and will run 2 months on one tank. I also have a generator to run everything.||||reg||4
Z||000014||Cliff Pennock||12-26-1999||06:34 PM||||Being a programmer for almost 25 years now, I was <B>very</B> concerned. I know exactly what <B>can</B> go wrong and what the consequences could be. If, at the turn of the century nothing serious happens, it's <B>not</B> because there wasn't anything to be worried about, but because most problems had been solved in time.<P>For us programmers it's a very frustrating situation that we will never be able to show people how serious the problem is/was, because if we do our jobs right nothing <B>will</B> happen and we will get a lot of I-told-you-so's. These people do not realize that the only reason nothing happened is because we worked our butts off to solve the problems.<P>The Y2K bug is/was a very serious one, of which only a system programmer can fully understand the implications. I'm not trying to scare anyone because I'm equally convinced that most problems with the major systems have been solved, or will be shut down at the turn of the century.<P>But let me tell you - if we hadn't done anything, the consequences would've been <B>far</B> more serious than you could ever imagine...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||12-26-1999||08:02 PM||||Cliff--Can we assume that there won't be any Y2K related problems with the Forum?  (Hey!  I've got my priorities straight!)<P>Seriously, though (I *can* be serious...sort of), fill up your gasoline tanks, make sure you have a weeks worth of groceries, a gallon of bottled water per person per day (per day of what?), get a hard copy of your bank account and make sure you have at least 2 weeks worth of any prescription medications.  (This is more important for guys like me and dogtired <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<BR>BTW, dog--Can you smell me all the way out there in the wheat fields?  Sorry!  I have to blame my sister-in-laws cooking! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||12-26-1999||08:43 PM||||Y2K......<P>It won't affect your car, except that some gas stations may close. Few ECM's track dates.<P>The North American Power Grid is considered fairly stable. It's not 100% but close. we're just as likely that some moron will whack a pole or tower. The big companies, like Conectiv, are mostly worried about the municipal and other small systems out there. these small systems can screw up the big ones. municipal systems are notiriously bad in general.<P>Most of the north American phone systems are now full digital. This should be interesting. Some PBX systems could go to blazes. Mostly tho I think it will be limited to the voice mail and billing systems going nuts. The "switches" themselves don't have to process allot of dates.<P>Contrary to popular belief the Y2K problem is not a programming bug but an intentional programming choice. It's biggest affects are to anything using a database (IRS, Banking, Accounting are prime victems) or to some systems have programed scheduals for maintinace(Nuclear systems and others).<P>This programming choice was made to save RAM and Disk space. Even as late as 1987 1mb of RAM was 1000$us or more. PC Hard disk sizes of 30-40mb where considered huge. When you could drop 2 characters of data from a million records or more the savings in computer hardware were enormous.<P>Your home computer will have very little problem. If you've applied the patches to win9x or NT it mostly fixes everything a home user needs. Win9x patches are available at <A HREF="http://windowsupdate.microsoft.com" TARGET=_blank>http://windowsupdate.microsoft.com</A>  or for win98 it's on your start button.<P>There is one other thing you should do by hand. Go into control panel, regional settings, date, and set the "short date" to M/d/yyyy. This is required for some things in Excell and Word to be fully Y2K according to microsoft. (you also need any Office patches that apply to your version) The problems are limited only to documents or spread sheets doing date calculation.<P>PC hardware. Most PC hardware built in the last few years is ok or can have new BIOS flashed into it. One of 2 BIOS failures are posible. Either you will have to manually set the date, or the BIOS will not allow the date. If you can manually set the date, the you also need to worry about feb 29, 2000. If you cannot set the date, and win98 cannot compensate, then you are likely useing a very old 486 or lower computer or a few early pentiums. There are a few software drivers that claim to fix all BIOS problems but I'd test one carefully before paying money.<P>Bussiness systems. The LAN is probly fine if the server is, and IF the winNT admin has any brains. PC's on a LAN can get thier time from the server. This is the default behavior on Novell Netware clients logging into a Netware server. Microsoft Netware client and Client for Microsoft Networking will only get server time if a "NET TIME \\sname /SET /YES" is executed. Most NT admins don't put the "net time" command into the login script.<P>WAN links between sites are as reliable as the telecomunications service they use. If Bell Atlantic or MCI choke then the WAN will too. I don't believe most routers do allot of date processing. I don't have the RFC's or Cisco doc's handy that cover that at the moment. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Monkeyman||12-27-1999||01:14 AM||||What he said!  And, bummer.  The IRS will be out of commission.  I'll be crying (NOT!).||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||12-28-1999||07:15 PM||||Seems to me the biggest Y2K problems are past us. Most of the problems occurred when they tested their fixes and the fixes shut everything down (sewer spillage, power killed to a few towns, locked up the ATM's).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||batboy||12-29-1999||12:05 AM||||For camping, I've been using propane lanterns and stoves for several years. I like it better than white gas, I think it's safer too. Anyway, during my Y2K preparations I noticed I was about out of propane, so I ran into Wal-Mart for a bottle. They were completely sold out! I managed to pick up a couple bottles at a local outdoors sports store, but apparently propane is very popular to stockpile for Y2K. FYI!||||reg||1
Z||000020||HotFieroGT||12-31-1999||10:37 PM||||I like batboy's idea of getting lucky with a nice.... no, I mean bad girl for new years!  My biggest worry about this whole Y2K thing is will she respect me in the morning! <p>[This message has been edited by HotFieroGT (edited 12-31-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||theogre||12-31-1999||11:48 PM||||Bats, others,<P>Check your lanterns that use Coleman Fuel. They May also use unleaded pump gas.<P>Personally I've used both "white gas" and propane. Never had a problem with either. I like the newer ones that also use pump unleaded best. Aside from having to presurize them every so often they are very cheap to run. Biggest problem I've ever seen is that people don't know how to take care of the mantles.<P>So far there have been no major reports of problems from the 0h0h bug. It's about 10:30EST(US) and there are still no major problems. So far the only problem I've seen at all is with the slot machines at Delaware Park and Dover Downs. Seems the maker of those slots told the state that the machines are Y2k but thay aint. Too bad if some dumb dink can't throw their money in a hole for a few hours.<P>Me? I'm stuck at work at this hour for this stupid crap. No. I'm not in a good mood.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Monkeyman||01-01-2000||04:17 AM||||Yawn!!  Y2K...What a ripoff.  No excitement whatsoever, unless you count all the drunks getting arrested.  I was looking for a little power outage at the least (just for a few minutes <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">).  I could've gone to bed. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000023||Patrick||01-01-2000||07:02 AM||||<BR>It turns out the dreaded Millennium Bug was a rather impotent insect...||||reg||6
Z||000024||Cliff Pennock||01-01-2000||09:30 AM||||With growing irritation I have been watching the news. Fortunately, no major Y2k problems have been reported so far. But what's pissing me off majorly: everyone is calling it a hype, a trap set by the computer industry, a waste of money.<P>The reason I'm so pissed at these people is because the only reason nothing (major) happened is because we've been working our butts off to <B>make sure</B> nothing happened. If we (the programmer's of this world) had done nothing, you probably would be living in a warzone pretty soon. I've already had my share of people telling me I was full of sh!t these past years because nothing happened.<P>There's nothing more frustrating than seeing a job well done, but nobody appreciating it. Oh well, that's today's society I guess...<P>Sorry for venting but every news broadcast I watch is making fun of us computer people.||||reg||8
Z||000025||batboy||01-01-2000||11:09 AM||||The same ones who are calling it a big hoax or hype are the same ones who stocked up the most supplies, so now they're mad because nothing happened. I say all the computer people should get a big hearty pat on the back! Well done! Thanks for saving us from the brink of diaster.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Dave85||01-01-2000||11:25 AM||||Speaking of stocking up, I'm a manager of an Aldi's grocery store and let me tell you, people did some major stocking up! Our store is wiped clean. I'm glad some people stocked up early because the last two days were he!!.<BR>BTW, thanks Cliff for a job well done.  I'm glad to see nothing happend.   <BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000027||William Federle||01-01-2000||11:48 AM||||I agree with the feelings expressed above that we should feel good that nothing bad happened. When you're warned ahead of time that something bad might happen and you successfully avoid it, you should be thankful to those who warned you and who helped you avoid the bad thing.||||reg||10
Z||000028||Cliff Pennock||01-01-2000||01:53 PM||||To be honest, it ain't over yet. The really big test will be the coming weeks. <B>Nobody</B> was really expecting something to happen on new year's day because there's no reason at all why a computer would shut down if it reaches the year 1900 instead of 2000 (it really is just another date). But what will be interesting to watch is what happens if on monday all (business) computers are switched back on again, and work resumes as normal. I suspect we'll be hearing the first stories of small glitches on monday.<P>Not even my own systems at the office are fully Y2K compliant. I already detected one computer who thinks it's Jan 4th 1980. Fortunately this was anticipated and the software is ignoring the system date.<P>Again, sorry for my earlier venting... Especially when it isn't even Fiero related!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000029||Phil||01-01-2000||02:39 PM||||Don't know if it's Y2K related or not but the little green lights and the lit candles on the forum don't work for me today.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Formula||01-01-2000||05:29 PM||||My moms computer went to Jan.4 1980 too.  I thought that was the date. My new HP went to Jan. 1. 2000, and my calender fell off the wall at midnight.<P>Cliff, I know how much programmers put into Y2K, I think you have the right to be angry at CNN, ABC,and NBC for saying it was all the programmers overestimating. I give thanks to anyone who helped the Y2K computer Glitch because we would be driving are fieros in WW3 right now if they didn't fix it.<P>Anyways, I finally took pictures of my car for its y2k aniversary. It was washed, waxed and detailed today for its pics. I 'll get them developed and scaned tommorow. WOOHOO.||||reg||1
Z||000031||fiero56||01-01-2000||06:19 PM||||I too was hoping for a little excitement. Even though I was willing to bet money nothing was going to happen, it would have been great to see times square or something else on tv lose all power at the stroke of midnight. Little power outage here would have been a little exciting too <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. <BR>Just think, all of our Fieros are another model year older. My car is now 12 years old!<BR>Glad there aren't any apparent major problems, yet. <BR>Good job Cliff, keep the world safe. You too Ogre. Have a great day everyone.||||reg||1
Z||000032||Monkeyman||01-01-2000||06:30 PM||||fiero56--Technically, our Fieros were a model year older back in the fall.  I know...I'm a pain in the A**!  Sorry, but I had to say something.  Nothing personal. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000033||Monkeyman||01-01-2000||06:33 PM||||Phil--it must be your computer.  They work just fine for me, thanks to Cliff.  Most of you know how much of an Internut I am.  I wasn't too worried about the Y2K thing, but I damn (I mean-darn) near sh** my pants worrying about whether or not I could get back on line! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000034||Patrick||01-01-2000||07:52 PM||||<BR>Cliff, I hope my comment made in good fun has not contributed to your irritation. If the work done by computer programmers around the world has helped to stamp out the Millennium Bug, great, more power to you guys. My beef is with the media. They did their very best to scare everyone earlier. The media have now turned around and are trying their best to make everyone feel foolish or stupid for listening to the warnings that the media themselves were promoting over the last several years. <P>As you already know Cliff, it's a no win situation for computer programmers. You'll simply have to sit back, smile, and feel confident in the knowledge that in some small (or large) way, you helped to prevent a possible catastrophe.||||reg||4
Z||000035||Cliff Pennock||01-01-2000||08:50 PM||||Patrick - no offense taken. My anger was directed to the media as well. But I got a little satisfaction today. I was watching one of the newsprograms here and they were again stating how much BS the computer-industry had been laying on them. So they had this telephone poll: Dial xxxxxxxx-1 if you think it was all a complete waste of money, or dial xxxxxxxx-2 if you think it was money well spent.<P>Well, at the end of the newsprogram (where they usually give the poll's result) they apologized for not being able to give the results... The computer had crashed due to  Y2K related bug! I laughed so hard - I almost pee'd my pants!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000036||theogre||01-01-2000||11:50 PM||||Well, I was online at midnight. From this very computer. At work. What a boring night.<P>So far the only thing I've seen break was some slot machines, and some cosmetic stuff on web sites. None of which any one really cares about when it comes down to it.<P>The "hype" as usual came from the world press who as usual don't know what they are talking about. Which make them look like fools they really are and now they are trying to cover their private places.<P>Edison Carter: When did the news become entertainment?<BR>Murray: Since it was invented.<P>The only thing the "news" is really good at it selling advertising space. Looked a a "news paper" lately? Count up the surface area given to "news" then measure the ad space.............. Ignoring the inserts completely.<P>Yes, the programers did a good job fixing things. No question there. What is already happinging tho is that some companies are looking to recover costs. This means a feeding frenzy in the courts is likely. To make things worse some companies replaced systems that the makers/vendors said fail Y2K when in reality thos systems where fine.<P>What will make the next couple days interesting isn't the 0h0hbug. It's seeing if harware that's been running 24x7 for months to years will spin back up after being shut down for the weekend. It's a common problem for hard disk drives that have run for a long time to not spin on a restart after being shut down and cooled. A couple months ago I replaced an entire drive array because a UPS failed to start and the drives got cold. The drives wouldn't spin up at all. They hadn't been shut off for years.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000037||DJRice||01-04-2000||11:24 AM||||Cliff, I think that you can take some stock in the fact that although the General Public will look back and think that y2k was an overhyped (new word?) event, those of us in the Information/Tech Business know that the result of the y2k bug is a much more efficient computing environment than has existed in previous years.  Most busineses felt there was no choice but to upgrade their systems or software.  Although it will become transparent, everyone will be able to benefit from the added security, efficiency, and even extra features now available becuase of the modernization efforts of the last couple of years.....||||reg||1
